WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.170
- Spider-Man: Far From Home
is out and, as of this taping,

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:07.220
is certified fresh at
92%, continuing the MCU's

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.230
marvelous rate of 23
consecutive fresh films.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.940
Not a single rotten, 21 of
which are certified fresh

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.400
but is it the best
Spider-Man movie of all time?

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.890
Hi, everyone, I'm Mark Ellis.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.250
That is my true identity and
welcome to the Aftershow.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.370
This is a spoiler-heavy
show, so if you haven't seen

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.710
the new Spider-Man movie
yet, I did my best.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.900
I am so excited to welcome
this panel to the show.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.770
First of all, we have Geek
Bomb host, Maude Garrett

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:33.610
is back.
- Hi!

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.533
- So glad to be here for this
one, especially, it's so good!

00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:38.260
- This is going to be a
fun conversation, for sure.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.680
And joining us Rotten
Tomatoes certified critic

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.930
and pop culture critic, the one and only,

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.400
Ms. Joelle Monique is here.
- Hi!

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.840
- I'm so excited to be here.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.380
- I am so excited to have
you and were we really

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.100
going to talk Spider-Man
without this next guy,

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.540
Scott Movie Mantz--
- Yes!

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.790
- Our returning champion is back.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.800
- Returning champion Spider-Mantz
meets Spider-Man, come on.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:00:59.990
We're going to have a great conversation

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.370
about Far From Home.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.160
Man, these Spider-Man movies are awesome.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.480
- They just keep getting
awesomer, according to

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.900
the Tomatometer, anyway,
so before we get into

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.830
our spoiler-heavy
conversation about all things

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.640
Spider-Man: Far From Home,
I do want to throw it

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.780
to my panelists individually
to just to get your quick take,

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.100
is this a fresh or rotten movie?

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.840
Joelle, start with you, fresh or rotten?

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.780
- Absolutely fresh, we're getting
our first mainstay villain

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.560
in a really long time.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.160
Tom continues to hit it
out of the park as, like,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.620
our sweet, adorable, like,
cherub, baby Spider-Man

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:27.673
and I love him.
- Yeah.

00:01:27.673 --> 00:01:30.320
- And, on top of that,
we get, like, a possibly

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.920
a new MJ angle which I'm
really excited to explore.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.290
- I am definitely with
you with the MJ angle.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.258
Scott Mantz, do I even
have to ask the question?

00:01:37.258 --> 00:01:41.570
- This is fresh by a mile and
you gotta look at this way,

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.570
not only did you have Spider-Man appearing

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.820
in Avengers: Infinity War,
and End Game, and Homecoming,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.530
and Into the Spider-Verse,
which won the Oscar

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.310
for best animated
feature but Far From Home

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.620
still feel likes a fresh, vibrant movie

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.540
and that's a feat unto itself.
- All right.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.542
- Maude Garrett, you agree
with your two co-panels?

00:02:00.542 --> 00:02:02.330
- Fresh, fresh, fresh, fresh!

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.870
This is like such a beautiful
movie, especially, like,

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.110
after the gravitas that was End Game,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.620
a lot of feels there
but we're kind of like,

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.610
coming out of it and
enjoying these so much.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.880
Jake Gyllenhaal, fantastic,
he's having the time

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.000
of his life.
- Yep.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.817
- I love Spider-Man and I
think that it's the best

00:02:17.817 --> 00:02:19.160
it's ever been.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.680
- I cannot believe that I'm
the only person on this show

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.500
that is going to say that
Spider-Man: Far From Home

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.770
is not rotten, I was just teasing y'all.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.360
Of course, it's a fresh movie.
(laughs)

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.870
I do have some issues with the movie.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.790
I have some things that
I want to get into,

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.300
the first of which is
the villain, Mysterio,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.800
because I've seen a lot of
people raving about how much

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.213
they love Jake Gyllenhaal in this movie,

00:02:37.213 --> 00:02:39.830
that this could be one
of the best MCU villains

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.140
that they've seen, so that's
some heavy company to be in,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.117
Maude, when you look at
somebody like a Thanos,

00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:45.380
or a Killmonger, or a Loki.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.768
Where does Mysterio rank as far as

00:02:47.768 --> 00:02:49.220
your favorite MCU villain?

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.790
- I honestly don't think
it's fair to kind of

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.580
put him in line with all those other ones.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.110
You've got Thanos, you've got, you know,

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.700
all of these villains
that have the greater good

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.500
kind of aspect where they
really do believe that

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.421
what their motive is is
the betterment of society.

00:03:02.421 --> 00:03:05.620
Jake Gyllenhaal and Mysterio
is a disgruntled employee.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.090
He's just an angry, angry
guy that feels like, maybe,

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.390
all the money, and success, and notoriety

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.020
shouldn't have gone to
someone like Tony Stark

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.622
and that he was treated
poorly and he's unifying

00:03:16.622 --> 00:03:19.710
disgruntled employee around
the world but using technology

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.260
to prove the point which
is like exactly really

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.170
what Tony Stark did using
technology to kind of gain power.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.020
But he's using it against him.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.640
- [Mark] Yeah, and, Scott,
we've seen this angle before

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.330
in the MCU as it relates
to Tony Stark and a former

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:32.997
disgruntled employee, so.
- Right.

00:03:32.997 --> 00:03:35.040
- Did you just see a lot of
shades of Justin Hammer here,

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.500
was this a little too Ultron-y for you,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.100
was this like, again,
we're mad at Tony Stark

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.580
and we have to form something
that gets our revenge?

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.870
- Okay, well, this is not
the first time we've seen

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.400
a disgruntled employee
in a superhero movie.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.440
Remember Amazing Spider-Man 2,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.960
Electro was a disgruntled
employee and remember,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.550
wasn't it Batman Forever
in which the Joker

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:55.870
was a disgruntled ex-employee?

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:57.880
- You're pulling out all
the comic book movie hits.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.720
- Right, but that's the
thing is that it's been done.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.670
What hasn't been done
is the use of Mysterio.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.350
Now, as a life-long
Spider-Man fan, I've loved how

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.410
they've gone back and they've
mined the classic villains

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.900
like The Green Goblin,
Sandman, you know, Electro

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.500
wasn't so hot but Doc Ock was great.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.580
But I've always felt like
Electro was underappreciated,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.930
overlooked as a villain,
he made is first appearance

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:23.620
in Amazing Spider-Man
number 13 back in 1964.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:27.090
And with today's special
effects in these movies,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.040
it's like the perfect
playhouse, a great wheelhouse

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.120
for a character like Mysterio.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.770
So, to update him as a
disgruntled ex-Stark employee,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.590
I thought, was a good
touch and Jake Gyllenhaal,

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:44.010
he was great but, at first,
it took me a little while

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:46.009
to warm up to him and here's why--

00:04:46.009 --> 00:04:47.557
- Smoke and mirrors, baby!
- Smoke and mirrors.

00:04:47.557 --> 00:04:50.130
- 'Cause you didn't really,
but this is a good thing

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.510
about the film, you
didn't know, in the movie,

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.520
what Mysterio's motive was.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.040
- Yeah, yeah, Joelle, I
want you to jump in on that

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.220
real quick because we, as
hardcore comic book fans or,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.420
at least, people that follow
the news, we know that

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.570
Jake Gyllenhaal has been cast
as this villainous presence

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:06.930
but I think that people
watching this movie,

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.610
my mom might watch this movie
and assume, oh, this is Jake,

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.470
puppy-dog-eye Gyllenhaal--
(laughs)

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.560
He's gonna be a good guy
teaming up with Spider-Man.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.340
- Yeah, I think that's what
really works about it, actually.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.940
I think we have had a lot
of disgruntled employee

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:23.410
kind of villains but they're
so angry and I feel like

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.790
he's not as angry at Tony
Stark as he is seeing

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.260
an opportunity to build himself.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.820
I think he's much less
disgruntled and more like,

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.110
I, too, am a genius and
I haven't had my moment

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.570
in the spotlight and
now I'm gonna seize it

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:34.790
because Tony Stark is gone.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:36.610
I also think it expands the world in such

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.770
an interesting way, you
now have regular people

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.790
who are like, I'm gonna get
in this superhero action.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.500
Whereas, before, everyone's
like, I kinda don't want to,

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.330
or I just have to stop
something bad, like,

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.230
the world is gonna end if I don't suit up.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.800
Whereas, now, we just
have guys who are like,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.940
I just have, like, some theatrical stuff.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.910
- But also, what's great,
and I agree with you,

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.990
I like how they just, sort
of, they've made it current,

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.400
they've made the character fit
by making him less of a hero,

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.610
more of a master of technology.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.380
But, true to the original
character of Mysterio,

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.100
he is the master of illusion
and there is a scene

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.490
in this movie where Mysterio
really messes with Spider-Man,

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.100
driving him bonkers that,
I swear, could have come

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.233
right out of a page from
Amazing Spider-Man number 67,

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.980
Stan Lee drama meter
and it is a great scene.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.810
And I'm sitting there watching
the movie going, like,

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.530
yeah, this is exactly how I
envisioned them using Mysterio

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:26.890
in a big MCU movie.

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:29.040
- Well, I'm watching that
scene myself and I'm saying,

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.313
this is such a worthy
opponent to all the powers

00:06:32.313 --> 00:06:34.910
that Spider-Man has, then
at the end of the movie,

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:36.870
Spider-Man just remembered
that he had Spidey-sense

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.040
and he built himself a suit--
(laughs)

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.570
You know, my favorite part
of the Mysterio Spider-Man

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:45.000
was actually the chemistry
that they had together

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.210
when he was being that
father-figure and then,

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.400
once Mysterio just manifested as a villain

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.820
by the end of the movie,
I just thought it was,

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.840
I don't want to use the word
forgettable because he did

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.090
bring some things to the
MCU but I don't think that

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:58.560
as we get farther into these
movies, I think we're going to

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.460
look at a first appearance for
a villain like a Killmonger

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.280
or a Loki, I think, are the two best ones

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.550
that we've seen yet, and
I think that Mysterio

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:11.190
might just be, he's not a
Malekith but he's just another--

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:12.023
- Okay.
- Another dude.

00:07:12.023 --> 00:07:15.000
- And you're saying that because,
maybe, you just don't have

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.880
the knowledge, maybe, of
the character that some

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:20.671
other people--
(guffaws)

00:07:20.671 --> 00:07:23.082
- Scott, wow!
- Probably, likely, yeah.

00:07:23.082 --> 00:07:24.865
- There you go.
- But I--

00:07:24.865 --> 00:07:28.790
- I disagree, just in that I
think that Mysterio is not,

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:30.960
because he's so different
than all the other villains,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.100
I don't think he's gonna
get, to me, like, Ultron

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.130
is one of the most forgettable villains.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.660
Like, he doesn't have--
- I agree.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.920
- Like a strong, central
motivation other than destroy.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.200
I feel like Mysterio's desire to be seen,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.550
especially as we've just let
all of the Avengers being like,

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.150
so that was wild, man, and we
gotta go like soul-searching

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.950
and we gotta get back to
our roots and we're not here

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.940
for the celebrity or even to be, you know,

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.940
spotlighted and lifted up,
we're just going to help

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.570
in the background, now,
is sort of, kind of

00:07:58.570 --> 00:07:59.980
where everyone's gone to.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.660
I think having villains
like Loki who are like,

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:06.450
I'm gonna shower down giant cloud-monsters

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.340
to destroy the earth, I
think it's going to have to

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:10.390
bring the Avengers back
out into the spotlight

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:12.650
and I think if Mysterio
is leading the charge,

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:14.200
especially knowing how charming he is,

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:17.710
knowing how good Marvel is as
revamping and angling, like,

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.880
I'll agree with that it might
have been a little bit light

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.886
but I think that's what
the MCU tends to do.

00:08:21.886 --> 00:08:22.719
They're like, well, we're
not quite sure what people

00:08:22.719 --> 00:08:24.570
will like, we'll right
something very strong

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.030
and then we'll let the
fans tell us what they like

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:27.400
and we'll surprise them later.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:30.410
- And it's also that MCU is
still rebounding the fans

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.435
from everything that went on in End Game.

00:08:32.435 --> 00:08:33.268
- So true.

00:08:33.268 --> 00:08:35.010
- We just had such a
heavy, really, two movies

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:36.970
with Infinity War and
the End Game and you put

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:40.280
Captain Marvel in the middle
of that and so, it was nice

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.480
just to have a movie like
Spider-Man: Far From Home

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.770
that has such a great sense
of humor and is so refreshing

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:46.820
in that regard.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:48.470
- I completely agree with you, Mark,

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:51.220
because here's the
beauty of Far From Home.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:54.730
So, first of all, it works as
a standalone Spider-Man film.

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:57.580
Second of all, I think,
it's a superior film

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:00.310
to 2017's Homecoming, okay.
- I agree.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:01.750
- And sec--
- And not many people do.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.000
- Well, it's a bit split.
- Yeah, it's a split.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.520
- But I definitely felt
like it was superior

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.270
in most of the ways because,
also, what this movie does,

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.830
it is a coda to Avengers: End Game.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:14.860
It extends phase three of the
MCU just a little bit further,

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:17.780
maybe inching towards phase
four just a little bit.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.220
Like, for people who just
wanted a little bit more

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.510
after End Game, which is a
heavy movie, a lot of deep,

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.640
emotional moments, here's a
movie that's just super fun.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:28.870
It's a perfect blend of
action, heart, and humor.

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.510
Perfect for the July 4th weekend.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.710
- Yeah, before we move
on to our namesake here,

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:33.960
I just want to get y'alls quick take.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.070
Does this kill the idea
of a multiverse because,

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.420
you know, it was teased
and now it's kind of

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:39.630
the fake backstory.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.840
So, does this mean we're
not getting a multiverse

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.449
anytime soon in the MCU?
- Not at all.

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:44.380
- Or does it mean that
it's actually more likely?

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.910
- Listen, again, looking at the structure

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:47.891
of typical Marvel movies, we look back at

00:09:47.891 --> 00:09:50.490
Captain America: The First
Avenger, we know that they like

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.360
to tease, like, here's some Thor stuff.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:53.640
It's just pictures, just
hanging in the background.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.820
I think mentioning it
actually means we're going to

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:56.970
get closer to it.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.300
I think, because you get Peter
pontificating of like, well,

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:01.450
how would that work and it
gives you six different ways

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:03.120
that it could possibly work.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.190
I think we're going to get
it sooner rather than later

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.461
and I think we're gonna come
back later to this movie and go

00:10:07.461 --> 00:10:08.632
that's where they were
teasing and that's where

00:10:08.632 --> 00:10:09.693
they were teasing it.

00:10:09.693 --> 00:10:11.360
- Maude, your body language says agree.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.090
- Absolutely, the fact
that they've said the word

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:14.610
means that it exists.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.940
I tried to get out of Amy Pascal

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.530
with into the Spider-Verse
the fact that the multiverse

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.120
is definitely in existence
and I was like, is the canon

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.650
and are we leading into
that and will we see that

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.470
split multiverse in an
upcoming Spider-Man movie,

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:29.667
to which she was incredibly tight-lipped.

00:10:29.667 --> 00:10:31.410
(laughs)
Of course.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.430
But this is the second
time we've really seen

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:34.430
or heard about it.

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:37.112
Even though it was smoke and
mirrors, I mean, hello that's--

00:10:37.112 --> 00:10:38.270
- That's Mysterio.
- That's it.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.440
- Yeah, I liked it a little
bit just as if nothing else

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.540
is a nod to what we
got with the, you know,

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:45.830
Into the Multiverse movie
but I think that we could be

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:48.330
seeing more of the multiverse
coming up in the MCU.

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:51.170
- I would also love to see a
live action Miles Morales--

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:52.130
- Yes, please!
- Spider-Man movie.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:54.420
- Female Doc Ock, in the
flesh would be great too.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:55.905
- Oh yeah, that would be awesome.

00:10:55.905 --> 00:10:57.060
- She was great in that.
- This could really go far.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:58.843
- Okay, well, while we pitch
more Spider-Man movies,

00:10:58.843 --> 00:11:00.590
let's move on to the Spider-Man
that we currently have

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:02.820
and we should be very happy
with and that would be

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.890
Tom Holland and, in this
movie, the way that Tom Holland

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.790
kind of tries to take the
mantle from Tony Stark,

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.316
I feel like Tony Stark is
always going to be a presence

00:11:11.316 --> 00:11:14.370
in these movies, going forward,
even though it's just going

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.900
to be the spirit of Tony
Stark living, breathing

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:19.340
through Spider-Man, is he
the successor to Tony Stark,

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:20.173
in your eyes, Scott?

00:11:20.173 --> 00:11:23.660
- Okay, well, first of all,
I agree that, in this film,

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:27.450
definitely feels not just
like Tony Stark is a presence,

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.650
it feels like he's in the
movie and he kind of is

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:32.240
in one particular scene.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.450
This movie is a love letter to
Tony Stark and it is a great

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.280
passing of the torch,
a passing of the baton

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:41.690
from Tony Stark who saw
Peter Parker as a great,

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:44.770
not just a protege but,
like, next in line to really

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.528
take charge of the Avengers and he's only,

00:11:46.528 --> 00:11:48.520
like you said, Mark, he's 16 years old.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.830
There's a whole lot of
places for this to go but,

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:55.050
I think with this film,
again, it's a coda to End Game

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.770
where we saw, again, spoiler
alert, this is a big one,

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.450
we saw Iron Man, he's gone, he's out.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:04.260
So, this sort of feels
like a further coda to him

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.930
and a tribute to his character
but, I think, after this,

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:09.610
you're going to see and feel
the presence of Tony Stark

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:11.360
much, much further in the rearview mirror.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:13.690
- It felt like the end of
End Game for a while there,

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.420
Joelle, but then, once
we get Spidey on the jet

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:17.810
and he's hanging out with
Happy and he's building

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:19.970
his own suit, it's like,
this is the new Tony Stark.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.760
Do you look at Spider-Man
as the new face of the MCU?

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:26.550
Should he be the new face of the MCU?

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.520
- Should he, I mean,
he's capable of doing it.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:30.420
I think people love Tom
Holland, they're having really

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:31.720
great reactions to him.

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.370
I think, what I really love about the MCU

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:39.370
is that it's sort of all-encompassing
but it also has like,

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:41.310
it's dealing with a lot
of adult themes and stuff.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:42.900
Spider-Man is still very young, to me.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.930
I think Tony leaving
weapons of mass destruction

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:47.380
to a 16 year old is--
(laughs sarcastically)

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:51.020
Questionable parenting.
- But it's so Tony.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:53.640
- But, that being said, so, I'll say this,

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:56.200
I'm not sure about
Spider-Man being the center

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:58.450
of the Marvel Universe,
he's your friendly,

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:00.344
neighborhood Spider-Man.

00:13:00.344 --> 00:13:01.177
- Not anymore.

00:13:01.177 --> 00:13:02.690
- How is he going to lead
the Avengers and how's Thor

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:04.040
going to take orders from Spider-Man?

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.130
And it's just a lot of weirdness
on that level, however,

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.250
if we're talking about
a spiritual inheritance

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.350
of the role Tony gave
which is sort of the heart

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.840
of the MCU, the guy people
turned to when they're not

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.250
quite sure how they're
feeling about something,

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:19.000
I think his decision to step
back, he took the exact same

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.600
route Tony took after Infinity War.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.230
He's like, I just want
to be with my family,

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:25.890
I'm not putting on this suit,
I'm just going to step away.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:28.010
I'll monitor things, I'm
gonna check in but, please,

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.270
don't involve me, and I think
that he was trying to achieve

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:32.420
what Tony achieved while he
was gone and I think that

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:35.470
makes him the spiritual
inheritor of Tony's legacy.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:38.150
- And if Tony Stark was the
spiritual leader of MCU,

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.180
I would still argue that
Captain America was the face

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.751
of the Avengers--
- He was the soul, yep.

00:13:41.751 --> 00:13:43.610
- And we had a Captain America cameo

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.500
and there's Anthony Mackie's
taking over for Captain America

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:48.090
but that got cut at the
end 'cause they didn't want

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.470
to undercut what was already
happening in the third scene,

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.480
so I would have loved
to have seen that cameo.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.770
So, maybe, hope for the Blu-ray.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:56.740
Do you think that Spider-Man
is the logical choice

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.340
to be the face of the MCU or do you have

00:13:58.340 --> 00:13:59.878
another candidate, Maude?

00:13:59.878 --> 00:14:02.330
- I want to step out from
sort of the law of it all

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:04.390
and the hypothetical
and I want to talk about

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:06.750
the Hollywood budgets
and that side of things.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:08.600
I think that you've got
Tom Holland who was brought

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.930
on board as the no-name
and that he is, now,

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:14.390
all-encompassing as a Marvel superhero.

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:15.800
I think that they can afford him,

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:18.020
I think that they've signed
him on to a 10 year deal.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:20.100
He, himself, has said
that he'll play Spider-Man

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:21.270
for the rest of his life.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.590
I think that the Tom Holland
is the new Hugh Jackman,

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:26.650
whereas, we are going to
see this guy play Spider-Man

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:28.070
for the next 16 years.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.450
Like, I think the tenure's
going to be very long

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.140
and established and
that's going to be great.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:33.978
Is he ever going to replace Tony Stark?

00:14:33.978 --> 00:14:34.811
- No.

00:14:34.811 --> 00:14:37.650
- No, we need the juxtaposition
between the ingrained heroes

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:39.950
that have the life
experience, that, you know,

00:14:41.166 --> 00:14:43.700
that the end of the world
is constantly on their mind

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:46.810
and then bringing it back
to what is human, like,

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:50.680
what's so human and what's
innocent which is Spider-Man

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.260
just wanting to kiss a girl that he likes.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:53.890
You need both of them.
- See, that's the thing.

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:55.480
- That's the thing,
Spider-Man still has a lot

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:56.313
of growing up to do.

00:14:56.313 --> 00:14:57.880
He's only 16 years old.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:00.330
We haven't seen him get the
job yet at the Daily Bugle

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.054
working for J. Jonah Jameson--
- That's not gonna happen!

00:15:02.054 --> 00:15:03.150
- That's not gonna happen, that's done.

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:04.340
- That's not gonna happen?
- No.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:06.190
- He's not gonna get a
job as a photographer

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:07.820
for the Daily Bugle?
- Because he's out already.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:09.867
- The first thing that happened
in that post-credit scene

00:15:09.867 --> 00:15:13.550
when J. Jonah Jameson is like,
"Peter Parker," dah-dah-dah--

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:15.290
- Which was, by they way, was awesome!

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:16.880
- It was awesome.
- But he knows his name.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:18.510
- This is why I really
don't have to work at all

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.520
as a host because--
(laughs boisterously)

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.460
Our next topic is, as a matter of fact,

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:25.840
that incredible post-credit
scene where you do have,

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:28.320
and it was good post-credit
scene anyway because it did give

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.840
Mysterio kind of a life
after supposed death

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.950
and we're like, oh god, he
revealed it, I can't believe it,

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:35.450
and then, at least the theater
that Joelle and I were in--

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:38.441
- Mnh-mnh.
- Exploded when J. Jameson--

00:15:38.441 --> 00:15:39.676
- Yes.
- That was great.

00:15:39.676 --> 00:15:41.299
- Played by J.K. Simmons
back on the screen,

00:15:41.299 --> 00:15:45.180
our theater was making so
much noise and rightfully so,

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.080
I couldn't really understand
where he was going with it

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.040
for a while and then I picked
up that he is anti-Spider-Man

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:51.960
and he's just very, kind
of, blogger-y, like,

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:55.050
I'm gonna attack Spider-Man
with this grain of salt

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.160
of possible truth that I have.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:00.870
So, I like where that was
going just as somebody

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:02.780
who wants to be entertained
in the moment but, now,

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:05.420
as we step back and look
at what this actually does

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.797
for the MCU, we know who
Spider-Man is now, Joelle,

00:16:07.797 --> 00:16:10.490
how is that gonna change?
- Everyone does.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:11.323
- What isn't gonna change?

00:16:11.323 --> 00:16:14.720
Everything changes, especially
because, here was I'll be

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:18.010
most interested to see is,
we know Peter does not want

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:21.290
anybody he's around to be hurt
and he says, consistently,

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.630
throughout the movie, if
my identity is revealed,

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:25.070
I'm not going to be doing this.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:27.510
So, do we come back and
Peter's like, nah, man,

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:28.600
I'm just Peter now?

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:31.170
But, also, Spider-Man has
such a strong relationship

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:33.060
with New York and it's
one of my favorite things

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.130
about Homecoming, which
is still my favorite

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.530
Spider-Man movie of
all time, was this idea

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:39.190
of how connected he is.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:42.040
How does New York let Peter
Parker just go by now?

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:45.040
Like, you are Spider-Man, so
it'll be interesting to see

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:46.960
and maybe that's the way
they force him to get

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.850
into the Avengers, if he skips The Bugle,

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.210
if he, you know, instead
of going to college,

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.770
he's got a ton of great
professors already around him

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:55.560
in the Avengers, maybe
they're training him up,

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:58.090
teaching him and he's, I don't
know, learning how to run

00:16:58.090 --> 00:16:59.600
Tony Stark Corporation?
(chuckles)

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:02.820
- But he's still a kid, he
needs to have the experience

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:04.660
of college to grow as a person.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:06.750
You know, yeah, sure, he's
around the best people

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:08.940
who could really mentor
him in the Avengers

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.144
but he need to grow as a
person to have the experience--

00:17:12.144 --> 00:17:14.550
- I hear what you're
saying but Generation Z,

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:16.410
as far as like, if you're
looking at kids right now

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:18.630
who are Spider-Man's age,
many of them are not going

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:19.970
to college, they just can't afford it.

00:17:19.970 --> 00:17:22.790
And so, maybe they lean more
into the current generation

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:24.258
of how they're navigating the world.

00:17:24.258 --> 00:17:25.091
- That's a great point.

00:17:25.091 --> 00:17:26.173
- They can't afford it or
they make hundreds of millions

00:17:26.173 --> 00:17:27.190
of dollars going right
the NBA and LeBron James

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:30.100
had done a pretty good job
of not going to college.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:30.940
- Or they're YouTubers--

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:32.640
- I would say LeBron James
is the best superhero

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.994
we have on earth right now,
he is our current Spider-Man

00:17:34.994 --> 00:17:37.990
and if you look at where
Peter Parker's going, Scott,

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:40.390
I think that, honestly, the
best use for him right now,

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:42.740
because I do agree with what
you said is that he is a kid

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:44.790
and he's not ready to handle
the responsibility that,

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.120
I think--
- With great power.

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:49.801
- That Anthony Mackie's, Falcon,
now, Captain America could

00:17:49.801 --> 00:17:51.380
or what T'Challa could.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:53.230
- What about T'Challa,
that's who I'm thinking.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:54.910
- But here's the thing, with
Spider-Man, because we know

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:58.270
who Peter Parker is, I think
he is a great distraction.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:17:59.810
I think that he is
something you can dangle

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:01.560
in front of villains and
they all go after him

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:03.500
and we have all these other
Avengers that can just

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.430
come out of the woodwork
and that's how you

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:06.770
take down a threat.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:09.140
- No matter what, no
matter what he grows into,

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:11.600
whether he goes to work
for the Daily Bugle--

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:12.690
- [Maude] It's not gonna
happen, he can't do it.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:14.610
- Whether he goes to
Empire State University,

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:17.400
whether he meets Gwen Stacy,
whether he starts dating

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:19.940
Betty Brant who they
introduced in Far From Home--

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.960
- How dare you, he's not gonna
date his best friend's ex.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.850
- Well, I mean--
- How dare you!

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:25.750
- Betty Brant--
- There are best friend rules.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.160
- Whose husband was the
Hobgoblin but I'm totally going

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:30.670
off the rails here, but the
other thing is, you know,

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:33.910
as much as I love Far From
Home, the problem I have

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:35.970
with the film, and this
is a problem, is that

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:39.370
while it's really cool to see
Spider-Man finally interact

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:41.750
and pal around with Iron
Man and Captain America

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:43.900
and the rest of the Avengers in the MCU,

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:45.960
there's something to be
said about having a movie

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.280
that's just about Spider-Man.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:50.360
I just want a movie about
Spider-Man which is why,

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:53.940
in terms of live action
movies, Spider-Man 2 from 2004

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:56.500
with Tobey Maguire, directed
by Sam Raimi, to me,

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:58.990
is still the best Spider-Man
movie of them all.

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.030
- I think that's why you're
not getting the standalone,

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:03.190
'cause we've already had
that, it's already been done

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:05.610
and I think that that was
a really poignant moment

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:07.960
that I had during the end
credits scene where it was like,

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.350
well, we know Spider-Man's
story so well because this is

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.130
the third iteration that we've seen.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:15.140
And I was ready, I was bracing
myself for when he does

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.470
become a photographer for the
Daily Bugle because it's like

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:20.160
that's the story that I
know and I'm ready for it.

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.360
So, to hear that that's
off the table, now,

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:25.157
we are now venturing
into mysterious ground.

00:19:25.157 --> 00:19:25.990
- Ha, ha.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:27.760
- Where this is like, you
know, unfamiliar territory.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:31.220
So I'm sad that we're not going
to see Tom Holland's version

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:34.030
of what that looks like
but we've got a whole new

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:35.860
stage here.
- How cool for the fans.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.480
- What keeps this movie from
being the best live action

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:41.510
movie for me though is
that it's such a busy film.

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:44.060
There's so much going on,
they're going all over Europe,

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:45.680
you have all these supporting characters

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:48.200
in Peter Parker's life, you
have all these other characters

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:51.705
in the MCU like, Happy
Hogan, and Nick Fury

00:19:51.705 --> 00:19:55.260
and there's so much story going
on, too, that I felt like,

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:58.220
at times, that Spider-Man was a guest star

00:19:58.220 --> 00:19:59.650
in his own movie.

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:01.580
And as a Spider-Man fan,

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:03.200
I just want a movie about Spider-Man.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:05.430
- Okay, what if they give
you a movie about Spider-Man,

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:08.370
possibly the next standalone
Spider-Man movie we get

00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:10.190
but that has Venom in it as well?

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:12.230
Because they could have Tom Hardy Venom.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:14.470
If you're gonna put J.K. Simmons back

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:17.180
as J. Jonah Jameson,
anything's possible now.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:18.820
So, Joelle, do you think that we could see

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:22.840
Tom Hardy's Venom go up
against Tom Holland's Spidey?

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:24.841
- Let me control my face.
(laughs)

00:20:24.841 --> 00:20:28.300
I am so sorry, fans, who
love this iteration of Venom.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:30.750
Your point and love are totally valid

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:34.250
and I respect them, oh, Lord,
I don't like that version

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:37.110
of Venom, I don't understand it.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:38.590
I think, we were talking a
little bit before the show

00:20:38.590 --> 00:20:41.340
about how, sort of,
expansive that character is

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:42.900
based off of how the Venom movie ended,

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:44.690
so maybe it could morph
into something else.

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:47.759
I'll say, Marvel could
do it; I hope they don't.

00:20:47.759 --> 00:20:48.967
(chuckles)

00:20:48.967 --> 00:20:50.190
- [Mark] I think they will do it, Scott.

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:52.570
- I think they will do it
and I think that, listen,

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:55.430
I did not like the standalone Venom movie

00:20:55.430 --> 00:21:00.430
but I did like, not love, but
I did think that Spider-Man 3

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:03.550
from 2007, not the best Spider-Man movie

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:04.940
but not the worst either.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.660
It has its moments.

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:08.782
Oh, come on, Maude!
(laughs)

00:21:08.782 --> 00:21:11.160
You know, you're giving
me the dark Peter Parker.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:15.200
Listen, that scene was a
bit much, but Eddie Brock

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.530
is played by--
- Topher Grace.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:18.990
- Topher Grace in the movie.
- My doppelganger, yes.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.470
- I thought that he was actually
really good but, I think,

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:23.940
obviously, there's a lot of
potential for a really great

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:27.210
Venom villain against
Spider-Man, that's coming.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:28.530
All the fans definitely want to see it,

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:31.548
they need to get that right
and I think that's coming soon.

00:21:31.548 --> 00:21:32.533
- But they're going to
make it into a bunny movie

00:21:32.533 --> 00:21:35.060
at the end 'cause everyone likes
Venom and they kind of like

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:37.020
that he's this, like--
- Anti-hero.

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:40.219
- Maude, is Venom a
villainous enough presence

00:21:40.219 --> 00:21:42.818
to go up against Spidey or
do you just want to see him--

00:21:42.818 --> 00:21:44.694
- I want Tom squared.
(laughs)

00:21:44.694 --> 00:21:48.252
I want to see Tom Holland
and I want to see Tom Hardy.

00:21:48.252 --> 00:21:50.057
And I want to see them Tom together.

00:21:50.057 --> 00:21:51.210
- [Scott] Tom Squared, ha!
(laughs)

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.310
- I just want some Tom time.

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:54.820
- I like Tom is being
used as a verb but ja-ren

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:56.510
to everything else, it's an adverb.

00:21:56.510 --> 00:21:58.627
- [Maude] It is Tom for that movie.

00:21:58.627 --> 00:22:00.300
(laughs)
(exclaims)

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:02.970
- It's Tom to move on to
the next post-credits scene

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:06.110
which was another twist
that we got that really blew

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:08.741
the MCU wide open because
we thought we were watching

00:22:08.741 --> 00:22:10.620
Nick and Maria.
(chuckles)

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:14.040
We weren't, we were watching
Skrulls play the role

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.070
and then Nick Fury's off doing other stuff

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:18.800
in more of a Captain Marvel
sort of setting, so--

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:20.300
- [Maude] He's having a holiday.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:21.510
- He's on--
- The man deserved it.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:23.686
- [Maude] He's done how many
movies already as Nick Fury?

00:22:23.686 --> 00:22:24.820
- He was having a fake holiday,
then he gets off the screen

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.530
then he says back to work,
so do you like that twist

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:29.780
that it wasn't actually
Nick Fury the entire time

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:31.090
in Far From Home, Maude Garrett?

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:32.870
- All right, so here's a
little thing that I'm noticing

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:35.030
with Marvel, we're starting
to fall into crutches

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:36.880
and these two things
that we've established

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:40.370
in the Marvel Universe, now,
can kind of solve any problem.

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:42.380
And that is the introduction of Skrulls

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:45.919
being shapeshifters and,
potentially, playing anyone

00:22:45.919 --> 00:22:48.830
which is good and bad
because the good thing is,

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:51.700
I, now, want to watch this
movie again and find these

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:54.670
quite, now, obvious hits that
it wasn't them, necessarily.

00:22:54.670 --> 00:22:57.080
Like, they had met before,
Nick Fury and Spider-Man,

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:58.007
and he said nice to meet you.

00:22:58.007 --> 00:22:59.610
And I was like, that's a little weird,

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:00.890
maybe I can't remember.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:02.850
So, it's like, I like those little payoffs

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:05.700
but at the same time, it's
like, someone's gonna die

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:06.960
and then they turn into a Skrull.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:08.850
And you're like, oh, cool.
- Right.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:10.600
- The other one is time travel, you know.

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:13.240
So we've seen that that's an easy fix,

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:15.631
so if you don't like it, go
back in time and do it again.

00:23:15.631 --> 00:23:19.050
So I think that these are two
very blanket, very clever,

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.964
but very forgiving kind of
crutches that they can lean into.

00:23:21.964 --> 00:23:22.960
- [Mark] Yeah, Scott, I
had the pleasure of seeing

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:25.432
this movie last week and,
so, I said I'm going to

00:23:25.432 --> 00:23:27.010
give myself the weekend and
then I'll know how I feel

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:29.360
about this post-credit
scene but I have no idea

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:30.430
how to feel; tell me how to feel.

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.330
- Okay, well, first of
all, I hate to say this but

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:36.760
the better credit scene was the big reveal

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:38.660
to the city of New York and to the world

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:40.260
that Peter Parker was Spider-Man.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:43.400
What came after that was
the reveal of Nick Fury

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:45.290
as a Skrull.

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:49.990
I didn't really care that much
about it because I did not

00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:52.620
care for the movie, Captain Marvel.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:55.520
I thought it was bland,
I thought it was weak,

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:57.440
and I just really didn't
think that the character

00:23:57.440 --> 00:23:58.273
really stood out.
(laughs)

00:23:58.273 --> 00:24:00.220
Sorry, I thought that--
(stammers)

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.548
- [Joelle] I thought
we were friends, Scott.

00:24:02.548 --> 00:24:03.690
- I'm gonna get a lot of
heat for this but I thought

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:07.155
that Captain Marvel was the
weakest movie in the MCU.

00:24:07.155 --> 00:24:10.370
(blows raspberry)
- Have seen Thor: Dark World?

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:13.260
- Thor: Dark World is also
not good and neither is

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:15.960
Iron Man 2 and neither is
Guardians of the Galaxy 2.

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:18.570
- Okay, so defend this
post-credits scene for us, Joelle,

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:19.520
because why--
- Listen.

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:22.110
- Why is this so important
that, oh, Nick Fury

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:23.760
is off doing what?

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.860
- Nick is our link across the MCU.

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:29.230
He is the thing that
brings us all together.

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:31.500
So, I was personally shocked
when people were like,

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:33.430
like, I knew, instantly, something
was wrong with Nick Fury.

00:24:33.430 --> 00:24:35.620
Like, Mysterio's antics,
not that great, like,

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:38.420
after the bar scene, there's
no reason, Nick Fury,

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:40.500
who's a thousand times smarter
than me isn't picking up

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:41.980
on these things, something is wrong.

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:43.500
And then, the whole time,
I'm freaking, I'm like,

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:45.750
what are they doing to Nick
Fury, like, he came back

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.320
and he's disoriented and
he doesn't have context,

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:50.020
and Spider-Man is the only
one he can reach out to.

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:52.610
There are still so many
people on the planet earth.

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:54.530
This is a setup or something
weird is happening,

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:55.560
I don't understand it.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:57.970
Then we get to the end and
there's just great reveal

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:01.420
of like, Nick is off-world,
he is setting up the Skrulls

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:03.710
to build or sustain something on earth,

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:04.750
we don't know what it is.

00:25:04.750 --> 00:25:06.110
What is your mission in out space,

00:25:06.110 --> 00:25:08.100
what are you doing out there,
do you need more heroes,

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:09.190
are you concerned, are you--

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:10.417
- Secret Wars.
- Oh!

00:25:11.815 --> 00:25:13.798
- Maybe, I'm not really, I
don't want to say Secret Wars.

00:25:13.798 --> 00:25:15.130
- I think we just won Mantz over.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:16.467
- Whoa.
(laughs)

00:25:16.467 --> 00:25:18.138
- Secret Wars.
- That's what's happening.

00:25:18.138 --> 00:25:19.845
- The Beyonder?
- Yes!

00:25:19.845 --> 00:25:22.720
- Oh man, mind blown!
(laughs)

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:25.130
(imitates gunshot)

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:27.417
- This is why we show up
and do these shows because--

00:25:27.417 --> 00:25:29.190
- Oh my god, I didn't thing about that.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:31.330
- [Mark] We have our
opinions, they can be changed

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:33.080
during the course of a conversation.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:36.050
- Oh my gosh.
- Well, there you go.

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:38.170
- Whether you're watching at
home or you're hosting the show

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.220
and you're not sure what Secret Wars is--

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:41.053
- [Scott] I gotcha.

00:25:41.053 --> 00:25:43.350
- Just give me quick
run-through from each panelist.

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:45.830
- Okay, back in the early
80s, 1984, to be exact,

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:47.780
the Marvel Universe was shaken up

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:49.990
when all the greatest
heroes like Spider-Man,

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:53.280
Fantastic Four, the Hulk,
and the greatest villains

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:56.123
like Doctor Doom and so on,
were taken to another world

00:25:56.123 --> 00:25:59.840
by a great being called
The Beyonder who made them

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:04.030
fight each other and whoever
won would have the powers

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:06.400
of the Beyonder and be able
to control the universe.

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:10.350
It is actually a story that,
in terms of the movies,

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:13.120
could be bigger in the stakes than even

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:17.200
Avengers: Infinity War and End
Game and I like the concept

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:19.720
of that's actually where
phase four could go.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:21.830
- So, basically, Spider-Man,
we know you used to love

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.500
being your friendly
neighborhood Spider-Man

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:25.890
and saving delis; it's gonna
get a little bigger than that.

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:27.124
(snickers)
(laughs)

00:26:27.124 --> 00:26:28.630
- We have one more topic
to get to and that would be

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:31.150
your overarching thought
about how we ended phase three

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:33.160
because this is the official
end of phase three, now,

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:34.560
as we get into phase four.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:37.890
And where does this rank in
your favorite Spider-Man films

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:38.790
of all time?

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:39.880
Joelle, I'll start with you.
- Okay.

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:43.590
- Fitting end to phase
three, the right movie

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:45.563
to launch phase four?

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:49.370
- I think if we had had
that Captain America scene,

00:26:49.370 --> 00:26:53.020
I would feel better about
this being the start of four.

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:54.760
To me, this was such a...

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.570
You caught like an addendum.

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:59.890
I don't like it, I don't like.

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:02.498
To me, I don't need a
post-credit scene movie.

00:27:02.498 --> 00:27:03.920
- I see what you're saying.
- You know what I mean?

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:06.100
- We had a definitive
ending and it was so good

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:10.010
and I came into this, partly,
where are we going with four?

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:11.500
- [Maude] It's literally
called End Game as well.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:14.280
- Exactly, that's the end
of it, so people were like,

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:15.700
it's like an extension of three.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:17.490
Well, who needs that,
like, why are we here?

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:19.560
I feel like, what I was
really hoping for was like,

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:22.240
what's the new direction, is
it Avengers, are Avengers like,

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:23.213
we don't have Tony anymore,
we need to start going

00:27:23.213 --> 00:27:26.360
in a new direction, is it
gonna be the teasing of X-Men?

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:28.380
And it turns out, it's
the teasing, possibly,

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:30.973
of Secret Wars but it, to me,

00:27:31.929 --> 00:27:34.330
it didn't connect to anything.

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:36.090
I still feel out in space, I
don't know what's happening

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:38.815
in phase four, I didn't really
need an ending for three.

00:27:38.815 --> 00:27:40.420
- I feel like it's the
exhale after being punched

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:41.580
in the gut.
- Bingo!

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:43.750
- So it's like, ho--
(gasps)

00:27:43.750 --> 00:27:44.583
All right we're back.
- I need a breath.

00:27:44.583 --> 00:27:46.700
- And I don't feel like it's a full stop.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:48.620
It's not like the end, it's not like,

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:51.120
it's almost like the
webbing, pun intended,

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:52.540
between the two.

00:27:52.540 --> 00:27:55.890
So it's like the inhale
that you get to back

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:56.723
into the next one.

00:27:56.723 --> 00:27:59.010
I don't see it as a start
end, you know, like,

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:00.110
it's not black and white like that.

00:28:00.110 --> 00:28:01.980
- I think it's something we'll
be able to measure better

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:04.790
five or six films out when
we can understand, again,

00:28:04.790 --> 00:28:07.630
the great thing about the
MCU is that it is developing

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:08.770
universal story telling.

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:11.010
It's been my obsession
since I was a child,

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:14.770
I love crossover, I love,
like, extending universes.

00:28:14.770 --> 00:28:17.030
It's why I like the fact that
we have like 8000 stories

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:18.750
in here because if you're an MCU die-hard

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:19.830
and you've seen all
these movies and you've

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:22.020
watched them again, these
stories are super easy to follow

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:23.380
because you're like, oh hey, Happy's here,

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:24.610
that's really cool.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:28.040
I think that rewatch-ability
of this movie is going to be

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.350
so high, there's so much
to go back and be like,

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:32.610
oh, I see that there and
like, I see how this connects

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:35.420
to our future stuff, so I'm
looking forward to our next

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:37.310
three movies coming and then
coming back to revisit this.

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:40.810
- Scott does rewatch-ability,
for you, make a great film?

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.020
So does that elevate Far
From Home above Homecoming,

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:44.910
is that why you have that ranking?

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:46.721
- Absolutely, rewatch-ability
makes a movie.

00:28:46.721 --> 00:28:51.330
I mean, I've seen Into the
Spider-Verse like four times now

00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:54.680
and there's so much to Into
the Spider-Verse, especially

00:28:54.680 --> 00:28:57.455
Easter eggs for MCU
fans and Spider-Man fans

00:28:57.455 --> 00:29:00.450
to find so many things and
also the depth of that film

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:02.530
which I saw as the It's A Wonderful Life

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:04.780
of Spider-Man movies
which we could talk about

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:07.840
on another Aftershow for
Into the Spider-Verse

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:09.450
which we could do, like, anyway.

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.690
But I saw Far From Home,
like I said, at the top

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:15.750
of the show, as a coda, as
an epilogue to End Game.

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:18.200
What you just said, Maude,
about it being sort of like

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.450
catching your breath after
being punched in the gut,

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.120
End Game was a very heavy movie,
a lot of emotional moments

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:24.177
and it ended--

00:29:24.177 --> 00:29:25.180
- [Maude] I cried for 20 minutes straight.

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:28.780
- It ended phase three in a
really emotional satisfying way,

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:31.860
you know, 22 movies, 11 years,
but this is sort of like

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:34.320
peeking ahead of what happens after that.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:36.180
I'd like to just know,
what happens after that?

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:38.400
And it doesn't really
advance into phase four

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:40.860
in any definitive way
except for, I guess, that

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:44.660
post-credit scene that I
like a whole lot more now

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.830
than I did 10 minutes ago.
(chuckles)

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:50.010
But I think it's going
to be a rewatch-able film

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:52.490
and, yeah, on first viewing,
I thought it was a very

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:55.210
busy movie but maybe I'll
like it better when I watch it

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:57.011
again and again.
- I just had an epiphany.

00:29:57.011 --> 00:29:58.240
(groans)
- Here we go.

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:02.090
So, we don't get Peter Parker
working for the Daily Bugle

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:05.103
because he's no longer
anonymous but guess who is?

00:30:05.103 --> 00:30:06.521
- Is it MJ?
- Mary Jane?

00:30:06.521 --> 00:30:08.430
- No, Miles Morales.
- Oh!

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:10.423
- What if Miles Morales gets
hired as a graphic designer?

00:30:13.178 --> 00:30:14.011
- Wow.
- Or the likes?

00:30:14.011 --> 00:30:15.630
- Oh my gosh.
- And works at the Bugle?

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:17.490
- [Scott] You are killing
me, you are killing me.

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:19.004
- [Mark] Joelle came
hard to the hole with her

00:30:19.004 --> 00:30:23.270
MJ prediction and then, you
topped it with Miles Morales.

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:24.103
So--
- Oh, my god.

00:30:24.103 --> 00:30:25.550
- And that's how we get Miles in there.

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:27.680
- Oh my god, this is like
the greatest Aftershow ever.

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:28.720
- [Joelle] Are you secretly
writing for Marvel?

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:31.130
- This is what we're doing
right now as fans of the MCU

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:33.790
is that we're looking into
phase four and saying, well,

00:30:33.790 --> 00:30:35.440
this is what we most want to see.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:36.770
One of the things I
would love to see in more

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:39.830
Spider-Man movie is more of
Mary Jane, I want to see more

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:42.780
of Zendaya because I thought
she was so good in this movie.

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:44.910
I think it was a giant leap
getting to know her character

00:30:44.910 --> 00:30:45.927
a little bit more--

00:30:45.927 --> 00:30:47.230
- And I kinda wish we had a
little bit more of that secrecy,

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:48.750
you know, like that
secret society that they

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:51.300
both have together and she's
holding the biggest secret

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:52.366
on her shoulders--

00:30:52.366 --> 00:30:53.644
- [Mark] And then, as soon as she knew it,

00:30:53.644 --> 00:30:54.734
it wasn't a secret anymore.

00:30:54.734 --> 00:30:55.960
- I definitely feel like
they did more with her

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:58.440
and had to cut out
because she has maybe two

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:02.880
really good character pivots
in the movie that we don't get

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.210
any kind of like lead in
to, she sort of just like,

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:07.550
and now I'm a spy and I was
like, dang it, could we have

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:09.210
had little teases of this earlier.

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:10.370
She's so great in the role--

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:13.680
- But we had so much work to
do setting up phase four though

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:14.749
so we got a lot--

00:31:14.749 --> 00:31:15.989
- [Joelle] It's true, I know
there's a lot happening.

00:31:15.989 --> 00:31:18.550
- So, if I give you one
event that need to happen

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:21.660
in phase four: is it
Secret Wars, is it X-Men?

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.230
Scott, I'll let you go first.
- Oh, man.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:25.450
- What's the one thing we
want to see in phase four?

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:27.660
- Well, I think the thing,
and listen, as much as I am

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:31.270
a diehard fan of Secret
Wars, I mean, I was like 15

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:33.270
when that come out in 1984.

00:31:33.270 --> 00:31:35.230
Yeah, I know, I'm old.
(laughs)

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:37.160
But I think the thing that
fans most want to see,

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:41.780
now that Disney bought Fox
is X-Men and Fantastic Four

00:31:41.780 --> 00:31:42.910
in the MCU.

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:45.460
And Kevin Feige, he president
of Marvel Studios said

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:47.900
that was still a few years
away, but that's what fans

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:49.650
really do want to see
and that's gonna happen

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:52.820
and they're gonna reboot
and recast the characters,

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:54.560
just like they did with
Tom Holland and Spider-Man.

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:56.150
- [Mark] So there's a
vote for X-Men; Joelle?

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:58.660
- I'm also going to give my vote to X-Men.

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:01.450
Storm is coming, I don't know
when, and I don't know where.

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:04.190
I know about four actresses
are competing for the role

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:06.240
already, just sight unseen,
like, we would just like it

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:07.073
when it's available.

00:32:07.073 --> 00:32:09.350
I'm secretly rooting for
Yetide to be Doc Ock, I guess,

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:10.560
not-so-secretly.

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:12.313
Yetide Badaki, she's amazing.

00:32:13.171 --> 00:32:14.850
I also think that you
have the Scarlet Witch

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:16.910
and this opportunity to
make her the beginning

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:18.350
and center of the X-Men.

00:32:18.350 --> 00:32:20.150
Again, what I just like
about the MCU so much is that

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:21.770
they do that great surprising of us.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:23.680
Like, they're just like, you
don't know what's gonna happen.

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:25.680
You think you do, we're gonna
give you things that you like

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:28.030
but we're just gonna mutate
them so that they're new,

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:31.240
and interesting, and fun, and
I feel like, with the X-Men,

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:34.030
it's just, there's so much potential.

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:35.880
And the fact that we
get so many great wars

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:37.840
between the X-Men and the
Avengers and that could be

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:39.930
coming up and we're
drawing new sides and then,

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:41.470
but I really want to
see is more fleshing out

00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:43.370
of these new characters, to be honest.

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:47.430
Kamala Khan should be
there and be amazing.

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:49.810
We should have America
Chavez needs to come in

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:51.900
and just kick so much butt.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:54.640
I'd love to see Silk,
there's just so many great,

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:57.870
young characters and I think
we could build a great,

00:32:57.870 --> 00:33:01.310
little high school young Avengers world.

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:02.530
I think it's what's been sorely missing

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:04.680
from superhero movies
for a long time and--

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:06.777
- [Maude] I know, DC needs
to do Teen Titans already.

00:33:06.777 --> 00:33:07.610
(sighs loudly)

00:33:07.610 --> 00:33:08.680
- I mean, their TV show's
really great though.

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:09.942
- It is, yeah.
- But, yes.

00:33:09.942 --> 00:33:12.443
- [Mark] There's that can of
worms to open in the comments.

00:33:12.443 --> 00:33:13.276
(laughs)

00:33:13.276 --> 00:33:14.930
Maude Garrett, what's the
one thing we need to see

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:15.763
in phase four?

00:33:15.763 --> 00:33:17.360
- All right, so I'm a
little bit overwhelmed

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:19.820
with introducing all
the X-Men and it's like,

00:33:19.820 --> 00:33:21.940
how are you going to do
that when we have so much

00:33:21.940 --> 00:33:23.980
happening already and I don't
want it to get convoluted,

00:33:23.980 --> 00:33:25.920
so here we go.
(chuckles)

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:28.250
Hold your head, 'cause
your mind's gonna blow.

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:30.540
We are introducing the multiverse

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:33.050
and in another universe,
they're not superheroes

00:33:33.050 --> 00:33:35.450
and supervillains,
they're just called X-Men.

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:37.313
And that's how we get the X-Men.

00:33:37.313 --> 00:33:38.146
- [Mark] Wow.

00:33:38.146 --> 00:33:38.979
- And that's how they're involved.

00:33:38.979 --> 00:33:40.720
So they're not, so it's just a multiverse

00:33:40.720 --> 00:33:43.100
with a whole new set of
heroes that are just, yeah--

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:44.950
- [Joelle] So now we're
following two separate stories

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:46.070
on two separate story lines?

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:48.260
- And then, they combine.
- And they crossover.

00:33:48.260 --> 00:33:50.470
- And you could probably, I mean--

00:33:50.470 --> 00:33:52.240
- So, you're having
people face themselves?

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:54.700
- Well, no, I don't think that
they would necessarily exist,

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:57.400
it's a multiverse but instead
of having your Avengers,

00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:59.126
you have your X-Men.
- Oh, I see.

00:33:59.126 --> 00:34:00.531
- And that's how they--

00:34:00.531 --> 00:34:02.090
- I feel like I'm in a dorm
room with Donald Sutherland

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:05.383
and I'm saying, so you're saying
one tiny atom on my finger

00:34:05.383 --> 00:34:07.920
could be a whole universe.
(laughs)

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:11.010
Our minds are blown and that's
all the time that we have,

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.040
today, on the Aftershow.

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:15.280
I want to thank my panelists
for not only an incredible,

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:17.880
spoiler-heavy review of
Spider-Man: Far From Home

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:20.280
but also giving us so much to feast on

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:22.570
for phase four and beyond of the MCU.

00:34:22.570 --> 00:34:25.090
And now it's your turn,
comment in the chat section

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:26.680
right now, here, on YouTube,
I know you're already

00:34:26.680 --> 00:34:27.513
probably doing it.

00:34:27.513 --> 00:34:31.210
Let us know your take on
everything you saw us debate today.

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:33.180
What did you love about Far
From Home, what did you want

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:35.520
to see do better, did
Mysterio live up to the hype,

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:38.250
where do you want to phase
four in the MCU go from here?

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:40.430
We love hearing from
y'all, so hit that bell

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:42.050
if you're watching us
on YouTube right now,

00:34:42.050 --> 00:34:43.850
that's gonna give you an
alert anytime we have a new

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:46.100
Aftershow drop and, of
course, subscribe right here

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:47.800
to the Rotten Tomatoes YouTube channel

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:49.330
for my expert panelists.

00:34:49.330 --> 00:34:50.917
I am merely Mark Ellis.
(laughs)

00:34:50.917 --> 00:34:52.480
And I'm not a Skrull.
(laughs boisterously)

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:55.272
Have a good one, we'll see you next time.

00:34:55.272 --> 00:34:57.939
(upbeat music)

